{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q16",
  "speechText": "For me, I start with least privilege. The agent should only have the tools it really needs. That's because the model should not have direct access to internal services or databases. Then I would expose a small set of backend tools. The model can decide which tool it needs. But the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, checks permission, executes the action, and returns a structured result. For sensitive actions, I would also require user confirmation when needed. For tracking, I keep safe logs with IDs and avoid sensitive data. For write actions, I use idempotency so retries do not create duplicates. So overall, the LLM can suggest, but the backend controls access, execution, and safety.",
